   126	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
   127	
   128	
   129	In recent years, Rust [ 42 ] has been widely used to build reliable
   130	software productively, with its unique design for security and performance. Rust enforces memory safety and type safety via the
   131	ownership mechanism without garbage collection [ 26, 35 ]. This
   132	makes Rust a great option for developing secure and efficient applications and frameworks, such as browsers, virtualization, database,
   133	and game software stack [ 2, 5, 10, 11, 19, 32, 48, 58 ]. Large projects,
   134	including Android and Linux, also integrate Rust into their main
   135	projects for security concerns. While the software ecosystem brings
   136	convenience to software development, it can also introduce potential reliability and security concerns to the software [33, 34].
   137	Although Rust is being increasingly adopted, the Rust ecosystem
   138	is still young, and many problems within the ecosystem have not
   139	been well studied. Previous studies on Rust security primarily focus
   140	on security threats caused by developers breaking Rust compiler
   141	security checks [ 8, 9, 24, 31, 45, 46, 59 ], but ignores the problems of
   142	the compiler itself. We observe that the compiler allows developers
   143	to use Rust unstable features (RUF) to extend the functionalities of
   144	the compiler. However, RUF may introduce vulnerabilities to Rust
   145	packages [ 13 ], and removed RUF will make packages using it suffer
   146	from compilation failure. Even worse, the compilation failure can
   147	propagate through package dependencies, causing potential threats
